 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The threshold falls between -0.5 V and -1.3 V at -250 µA. On-resistance is guaranteed at three gate voltages: 50 mΩ max at -10 V, 60 mΩ at -4.5 V and 85 mΩ at -2.5 V. The gate may see ±12 V at most and leaks at most ±100 nA there. Gate charge to -10 V is 14 nC typical, 7 nC to -4.5 V, and the internal gate resistance is 4 to 12 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat and current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The thermal figures belong to a 1 in² pad of 2 oz copper on FR-4 in still 25 °C air. On that pad junction-to-ambient resistance is 90 °C/W max for periods up to 10 s and 125 °C/W max held steady; junction to lead is 80 °C/W max. The 1.4 W dissipation (0.9 W at 70 °C) is worked from a 150 °C junction limit and the 10 s figure. Normalized on-resistance reaches about 1.7 at 150 °C junction against 1.0 at 25 °C (Figure 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulsed current and safe operating area",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Pulsed drain current is -27 A, repetitive, bounded by the 150 °C junction limit, at frequencies and duty cycles low enough that each pulse starts from TJ = 25 °C. Figures 9 to 11 rest on the junction-to-ambient impedance of the same 1 in² board and a 150 °C junction limit; the safe operating area is a single-pulse rating. Characteristic curves 1 to 6 are taken with pulses shorter than 300 µs at no more than 0.5 % duty."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Body diode",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The integral diode carries up to -2 A continuously, drops -0.7 V typical (-1 V max) at -1 A, and recovers in 11 ns with 3.5 nC of charge when switched off from -4 A at 100 A/µs."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Test methods",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Gate charge is measured by feeding the gate a current pulse Ig against a fixed drain supply, with Qgs, Qgd and Qg read off the gate voltage as it climbs to -10 V. Switching times come from a resistive load RL and a gate resistor Rg, timed at the 10 % and 90 % points of the gate and drain waveforms. Avalanche energy uses an unclamped inductor L, and diode recovery an inductive load with the DUT's diode commutated at a set dI/dt."
  }
 ],
